 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "**How they get into templates**\n\n- `src/tessel/app.py:493` – the Jinja environment creation (the method that builds `rv = self.jinja_environment(...)`) sets `request`, `session`, `g` (plus `config`, `link_for`, `get_flashed_messages`) as `rv.globals`, so imported templates see the proxies too.\n- `src/tessel/templating.py:21` – `_base_template_vars()` is the default context injector. It replaces `g` and `request` with the concrete objects for speed. `session` stays a proxy, so access is tracked.\n- `src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:184` – `template_ctx_injectors` is seeded with `{None: [_base_template_vars]}`.\n- `src/tessel/app.py:588` – `Tessel.fill_template_scope()` runs the app-level injectors (key `None`), then those of the request's bundles. Values passed to `render_template` take precedence. It is called from `templating.py:125` and `:167` (the render paths).\n\n**Adding your own variables**\n\n- Decorate a function that returns a dict with `@app.ctx_injector` (`Scaffold.ctx_injector`, `src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:550`). On a bundle it only applies to that bundle's templates.\n- Use `@bundle.app_ctx_injector` (`src/tessel/sansio/bundles.py:644`) for a bundle-registered injector that applies to every template.\n\n```python\n@app.ctx_injector\ndef inject_site():\n    return {\"site_name\": \"Tessel\"}\n```\n\n(`@app.shell_ctx_injector` is a separate decorator for the interactive shell, not templates.)",
